WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.690
Place a workload by evaluating its data, latency, capability, scale, and dependencies.

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:10.537
Sensitive documents may need to remain on approved local systems.

00:00:10.537 --> 00:00:15.384
An interactive assistant may require low latency near the user.

00:00:15.384 --> 00:00:21.918
A large batch job may tolerate network delay and benefit from temporary cloud GPUs.

00:00:21.918 --> 00:00:32.244
A long-context request may need a hosted model if local memory cannot support it, but only when the input is permitted to leave.

00:00:32.244 --> 00:00:34.984
Coding assistance illustrates the trade.

00:00:34.984 --> 00:00:44.467
Public code or generic questions may use either environment, while proprietary repositories may require local processing or an approved private endpoint.

00:00:44.467 --> 00:00:49.736
Document review can separate local extraction and redaction from external generation.

00:00:49.736 --> 00:01:00.906
Vision workloads may stay at the edge when images are sensitive or bandwidth is limited, while large offline image batches move to controlled cloud capacity.

00:01:00.906 --> 00:01:08.282
Retrieval-augmented generation has several placement points: source documents, chunking, embeddings, vector search, prompt assembly, and generation.

00:01:08.282 --> 00:01:11.443
Each can create copies or metadata.

00:01:11.443 --> 00:01:22.613
Agent workflows add tools, credentials, memory, and side effects, so placement must consider what the agent can access, not only where the language model runs.

00:01:22.613 --> 00:01:26.195
Use a decision record for each workload.

00:01:26.195 --> 00:01:35.257
State allowed data classes, required model capabilities, latency and availability targets, expected volume, cost limit, logging policy, and approved fallback.

00:01:35.257 --> 00:01:38.840
Revisit placement when requirements or providers change.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:47.059
The correct answer can differ between tasks inside one application; that is the purpose of intentional hybrid design.
